cal), but weak memory for numbers (attention), makes nasty remarks about colleagues (crew cooperation, professional communication). So strengths: reliability, stress, technical. Weaknesses: cooperation, communication, attention.\n\nCandidate C: makes correct decisions quickly (decision quality), keeps calm in crisis (stress), understands complicated technology (technical), difficulty communicating ideas (communication), regarded as egocentric (cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). So strengths: decision, stress, technical. Weaknesses: communication, cooperation, adaptability.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress/resilience), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), but regarded as know-it-all (cooperation/communication), has hot temper (stress/cooperation). So strengths: problem solving, responsibility, concentration. Weaknesses: attitude, temper.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. All have some issues. Which candidate has most balanced strengths? B has reliability and stress but poor teamwork. C has decision and stress but poor communication. D has responsibility and problem solving but temper. A has technical and leadership but not open to ideas and show-off.\n\nProbably B's conscientiousness and stress handling are crucial, but nasty remarks are serious for crew cooperation. That may be disqualifying. C's egocentric and communication issues also problematic. D's hot temper also problematic. A's not open to new ideas may affect adaptability.\n\nWhich is least severe? Maybe D: responsibility, concentration, problem solving are key for long-distance. Hot temper is concerning but maybe manageable? Show-off vs hot temper vs nasty remarks vs egocentric. Which is more safety-critical? Crew cooperation is vital. Nasty remarks could degrade safety. Hot temper could also degrade. Show-off maybe less severe.\n\nThus I might recommend Candidate A, despite not open to new ideas, because technical competence, anticipation, leadership, spatial vision are critical for long-distance. But need to address lack of openness. Could be mitigated.\n\nLet's decide: I will recommend Candidate A.\n\nNow produce public message: state recommendation, refer to no prior discussion, present facts: anticipates dangerous situations, excellent spatial vision, leadership, but also show-off and not open to new ideas, argue that strengths outweigh weaknesses.\n\nOk."}
